Z||000000||MoneypitGT||01-19-2001||10:32 PM||||A friend of mine has an '87GT 5spd in awesome shape, just turned 100,000 and he can't figure out what his problem is. When the car is cold and then started, about 5 mintues after it is started there is a loud clunk under the car, in the area of the gas tank. It only does it once and doesn't matter what you are doing driving, idling, accelerating. It only started recently and has no other problems, runs great. Has anyone else had this prob?<BR>   Chris<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fieroproject/moneypitgt.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||daved||01-19-2001||10:38 PM||||Try loosening the gas cap a bit to see if the gas tank is being pressurized (or sucking a vacuum!)too much and "oil-canning".  If it is....cant think how to trouble shoot that.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero Fanatic||01-19-2001||10:44 PM||||Ready 4 Rebuild.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-19-2001||11:56 PM||||Do what daved said. leave the gas cap off or loose and see if it stops.<P>If it does there is a problem in the vent line or the vapor canister. Disconnect the vent line from the canister and tighten the cap.<P>If the problem returns the line is blocked. If the problem does not return the canister is messed up.<P>If the canister is missing or disconnected make sure the vent line has not been plugged.<P>The canister is not horibly expencive. It actually pays for it's existance. It keeps gas in you tank from evaporating to the air. It traps the fumes and gives them up to the motor. In hot weather this can be a substantial amount of gas.||||reg||1
